To send a Node.js email calendar invite, render the email body with Unlayer Elements, generate an iCalendar file with ics, and pass both to Nodemailer. Keep them tied to one event record so the date in the message and the date saved to the recipient’s calendar don’t drift apart.


We’ll build a confirmation for an already-booked workshop. The first version writes an HTML preview and an .ics file without contacting an email server. A separate, explicit send step adds SMTP. Here, “invite” means an add-to-calendar event using METHOD:PUBLISH, not a meeting request with an RSVP lifecycle.


## What is the smallest useful implementation?


Use Node.js 20 or later in a project with a package.json. Install the rendering, calendar and sending packages, plus the TypeScript runner:


```text
npm     install   @unlayer/react-elements react react-dom ics nodemailer
npm     install   -D tsx
```


[Elements requires React and React DOM 18 or later](https://docs.unlayer.com/elements/react/installation) .


[Nodemailer’s current major requires Node.js 20 or later](https://nodemailer.com/) . Keep your lockfile. This is the code-first Elements library, not an embedded drag-and-drop editor.


Create confirmation.tsx. The explicit React import also supports projects using the classic JSX transform.


[tsx runs TypeScript files from the command line](https://tsx.hirok.io/getting-started) ; it is not a substitute for your project’s type checking.


```text
import     React     from     'react'  ;
import     {   mkdir  ,   writeFile   }     from     'node:fs/promises'  ;
import     {   createEvent   }     from     'ics'  ;
import     nodemailer     from     'nodemailer'  ;
import     {
Email  ,     Row  ,     Column  ,     Heading  ,     Paragraph  ,     Button  ,   renderToHtmlParts  ,
}     from     '@unlayer/react-elements'  ;


const   event   =     {
uid  :     'reliable-webhooks-20261112@events.example.com'  ,
title  :     'Building Reliable Webhooks'  ,
startsAt  :     new     Date  (  '2026-11-12T14:00:00Z'  )  ,
durationMinutes  :     60  ,
location  :     'Online workshop'  ,
url  :     'https://events.example.com/workshops/reliable-webhooks'  ,
}  ;
const   name   =     'Morgan'  ;
const   when   =     new     Intl  .  DateTimeFormat  (  'en-GB'  ,     {
timeZone  :     'Europe/Berlin'  ,     weekday  :     'long'  ,     year  :     'numeric'  ,
month  :     'long'  ,     day  :     'numeric'  ,     hour  :     'numeric'  ,     minute  :     'numeric'  ,
timeZoneName  :     'short'  ,
}  )  .  format  (  event  .  startsAt  )  ;


const   tree   =     (
<  Email   backgroundColor  =  "#edf1f5"   contentWidth  =  "600px"
previewText  =  "Your workshop place is confirmed."
fontFamily  =  {  {     label  :     'Arial'  ,     value  :     'Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif'     }  }  >
<  Row   backgroundColor  =  "#163b3c"   padding  =  "28px 32px"  >  <  Column  >
<  Paragraph   color  =  "#b3ddd2"   fontSize  =  "14px"  >  FIELDNOTE     LABS  <  /  Paragraph  >
<  Heading   headingType  =  "h1"   color  =  "#ffffff"   fontSize  =  "30px"  >
You’re   on the list
<  /  Heading  >
<  /  Column  >  <  /  Row  >
<  Row   backgroundColor  =  "#ffffff"   padding  =  "24px 32px"  >  <  Column  >
<  Paragraph  >  Hi     {  name  }  ,   your place is confirmed   for  :  <  /  Paragraph  >
<  Heading   headingType  =  "h2"   color  =  "#163b3c"   fontSize  =  "24px"  >
{  event  .  title  }
<  /  Heading  >
<  Paragraph  >  {  when  }  <  /  Paragraph  >
<  Paragraph  >  Duration  :     {  event  .  durationMinutes  }   minutes  <  /  Paragraph  >
<  Paragraph  >  Location  :     {  event  .  location  }  <  /  Paragraph  >
<  Button   href  =  {  event  .  url  }   backgroundColor  =  "#b8eed7"   color  =  "#163b3c"  >
View   event details
<  /  Button  >
<  Paragraph   fontSize  =  "14px"   color  =  "#53686a"  >
Save   the calendar file to keep the time handy  .
<  /  Paragraph  >
<  Paragraph   fontSize  =  "13px"   color  =  "#53686a"  >
Questions   before the workshop  ?     Reply   to   this   email  .
<  /  Paragraph  >
<  /  Column  >  <  /  Row  >
<  /  Email  >
)  ;
const     {   head  ,   body   }     =     renderToHtmlParts  (  tree  )  ;
const   html   =     `  <!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head>  ${  head  }  </head>  ${  body  }  </html>  `  ;
const   text   =     [
`  Hi   ${  name  }  , your place is confirmed for   ${  event  .  title  }  .  `  ,
when  ,     `  Duration:   ${  event  .  durationMinutes  }   minutes  `  ,
`  Location:   ${  event  .  location  }  `  ,     `  Event details:   ${  event  .  url  }  `  ,
'Save the calendar file to keep the time handy.'  ,
'Questions before the workshop? Reply to this email.'  ,
]  .  join  (  '\n\n'  )  ;
const     {   error  ,     value  :   calendar   }     =     createEvent  (  {
uid  :   event  .  uid  ,     title  :   event  .  title  ,
start  :   event  .  startsAt  .  getTime  (  )  ,
startInputType  :     'utc'  ,     startOutputType  :     'utc'  ,
duration  :     {     minutes  :   event  .  durationMinutes     }  ,
location  :   event  .  location  ,     url  :   event  .  url  ,
description  :     'Your workshop place is confirmed. See the event page for details.'  ,
status  :     'CONFIRMED'  ,     method  :     'PUBLISH'  ,
}  )  ;
if     (  error   ||     !  calendar  )     throw     new     Error  (  'Calendar generation failed'  )  ;


async     function     main  (  )     {
await     mkdir  (  'out'  ,     {     recursive  :     true     }  )  ;
await     writeFile  (  'out/confirmation.html'  ,   html  ,     'utf8'  )  ;
await     writeFile  (  'out/confirmation.txt'  ,   text  ,     'utf8'  )  ;
await     writeFile  (  'out/workshop.ics'  ,   calendar  !  ,     'utf8'  )  ;
console  .  log  (  'Preview files written to out/'  )  ;
}
main  (  )  .  catch  (  error     =>     {
console  .  error  (  error  )  ;
process  .  exitCode     =     1  ;
}  )  ;
```


Run the preview-only version:


```text
npx tsx confirmation.tsx
```


Open out/confirmation.html in a browser. Open out/workshop.ics with a calendar application and review the title, start time, duration and event URL before saving it. The text file gives you a readable alternative to the HTML email. No SMTP credentials are needed for this step.


