## Why keep calendar data outside the React layout?


Elements handles presentation. Its


[renderToHtmlParts API](https://docs.unlayer.com/elements/react/rendering-api) returns head and body; the example composes them into an HTML document exactly as documented. The layout follows Email → Row → Column → content. The plain-text body is authored explicitly from the same event values.


The


[ics API](https://github.com/adamgibbons/ics) creates the calendar string separately. Without a callback, createEvent returns { error, value }. Don’t manually concatenate calendar lines around untrusted titles or descriptions. Let the calendar library handle the file format, then handle a generation error before attempting delivery.


A stable uid identifies the event. Replace the example domain with a namespace you control, and store that identifier with your event record. Don’t generate a new identifier for every recipient or every retry. A UID identifies calendar content; it is not an email deduplication key or a guarantee that every calendar application merges repeated imports.


## How do you keep the time consistent?


The event begins at an explicit UTC instant. The calendar uses that instant; the email displays it in the chosen Europe/Berlin zone through


[Intl.DateTimeFormat](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Intl/DateTimeFormat) . The formatter chooses the offset for the event date, not for the day the email happens to be sent.


Avoid a bare date-time such as 2026-11-12T14:00:00 when you mean UTC. Likewise, don’t take a displayed local hour and label it UTC. If your booking system accepts local times, resolve them with the event’s time zone before preparing this record, including a policy for ambiguous or nonexistent times around daylight-saving changes.


For recipient-specific display, choose a validated, saved time zone instead of consulting the worker machine’s locale. The calendar application can show the UTC event in its own local zone. Different displayed hours can represent the same instant.


## How do you attach the calendar file and send one email?


[Nodemailer’s calendar option](https://nodemailer.com/message/calendar-events) accepts the generated string through icalEvent.content. Its method must match the calendar’s own METHOD. We use PUBLISH in both places because this workflow shares an event without requesting a response.


Add the following function above main(), then add


` if (process.argv.includes('--send')) await send();


` at the end of main(), after the preview-file writes:


```text
function     required  (  key  :   string  )     {
const   value   =   process  .  env  [  key  ]  ;
if     (  !  value  )     throw     new     Error  (  `  Missing   ${  key  }  `  )  ;
return   value  ;
}
async     function     send  (  )     {
const   transporter   =   nodemailer  .  createTransport  (  {
host  :     required  (  'SMTP_HOST'  )  ,     port  :     587  ,
secure  :     false  ,     requireTLS  :     true  ,
auth  :     {     user  :     required  (  'SMTP_USER'  )  ,     pass  :     required  (  'SMTP_PASS'  )     }  ,
disableFileAccess  :     true  ,     disableUrlAccess  :     true  ,
}  )  ;
const   info   =     await   transporter  .  sendMail  (  {
from  :     required  (  'MAIL_FROM'  )  ,     to  :     required  (  'MAIL_TO'  )  ,
subject  :     `  Your place is confirmed:   ${  event  .  title  }  `  ,
html  ,   text  ,
icalEvent  :     {     filename  :     'workshop.ics'  ,     method  :     'PUBLISH'  ,     content  :   calendar  !     }  ,
}  )  ;
if     (  info  .  rejected  .  length     ||     !  info  .  accepted  .  length  )     {
throw     new     Error  (  'Recipient acceptance needs review'  )  ;
}
console  .  log  (  'SMTP accepted the message:'  ,   info  .  messageId  )  ;
}
```


Set SMTP_HOST, SMTP_USER and SMTP_PASS using your SMTP provider’s credentials. Set MAIL_FROM to a sender the provider allows and MAIL_TO to one mailbox you control. Supply these as server environment variables, not checked-in source. Use a monitored reply address because the footer invites replies.


This configuration requires port 587 with STARTTLS. If your provider requires port 465, use its documented settings with secure: true instead. Don’t disable certificate checking to get past a connection error. Nodemailer’s


[SMTP reference](https://nodemailer.com/smtp) explains both modes.


After replacing the event URL and branding, and reviewing the preview files, opt into submission:


```text
npx tsx confirmation.tsx --send
```


Running the command again attempts another email. SMTP acceptance is not proof of inbox delivery, and an exception after submission can leave the outcome uncertain. Keep this manual first version separate from a retrying booking webhook.


## What belongs in the production workflow?


Prepare the message only after your application confirms the booking and selects its authorized recipient. Don’t expose this script as a public endpoint accepting arbitrary recipients or calendar URLs. Validate external event data before rendering, including valid dates, a positive bounded duration, required names and allowed HTTPS destinations. Keep sensitive join credentials behind an authenticated event page.


Before automating, persist an event revision and the prepared message payload. Use an application-level notification key for the booking and confirmation type, coordinate workers, and record submission state. Reconcile ambiguous SMTP outcomes before retrying; neither the calendar UID nor Nodemailer supplies exactly-once delivery.


Keep the MIME structure simple. Nodemailer recommends text, HTML and a single icalEvent rather than unrelated attachments. Review the received message and calendar import in the clients your attendees use. A browser preview cannot establish Gmail, Outlook or Apple Calendar behavior.


Our


[editable appointment-reminder tutorial](https://unlayer.com/blog/editable-appointment-reminder-emails-unlayer) covers a different need: letting customers edit reminder content in the visual builder. This implementation stays code-first. If reminders are next, use the


[event-reminder guide](https://unlayer.com/blog/event-reminder-email) for content ideas without treating it as a scheduler.


## Frequently asked questions


### Will this show Accept and Decline buttons?


That is not the contract here. PUBLISH shares an event without requesting a response. A meeting-request workflow uses REQUEST and needs organizer, attendee and response handling. Client presentation still varies. Don’t change the method alone and call the result a complete RSVP system.


### Does Unlayer generate the ICS file?


No. Elements renders the email content. The ics package generates calendar data, and Nodemailer builds and submits the message. Booking eligibility, recipients and delivery state remain application responsibilities.


### Can I update or cancel the workshop later?


Not with this first version. Updates and cancellations need a deliberate calendar lifecycle, stable identity, revision handling and appropriate method/status changes. Keep those separate from sending the initial confirmation.


